## Imagine-mode disclaimer

This ticket is design dialogue, not commitment. It captures a
**future territory** the author sketched after a v2 panel-of-four
verify prompt landed and was tested (ticket 0223, closed
superseded). Decisions inside this body are *proposals*, subject to
ratification. No code lands on main from this ticket until each
step is independently agreed and ticketed.

The Exp 2 §4 exhibit for the conference push remains
**1-round self-verify, 3 reps, 4 models** (the locked 0214 / Annex C
policy with the v1 VERIFY_REPLY).

## Context

The §2 quality bar names four dimensions: accuracy, coherence,
provenance, temporality. The §3 capability ladder explains why the
parametric baseline (§1), the deep-research frontier (§4), and the
tailored solution (§5) hit progressively more of those dimensions.
§5 is sketched but not designed; **0224 is the design**.

A full Imperial-Dragon-Harness (IDH) verification cycle would
sequence five stages, each adding rigor at the cost of compute:

### i — Bibliographic scoping study

Before the agent attempts the inventory, do a scoping pass over
the literature / source landscape:

- What primary databases exist (PDP8 official, MOIT decisions, EVN
  reports, GEM, IEA, Enerdata, JETP filings, news media)?
- What's their coverage, freshness, language, gating?
- What search strategy will the inventory pipeline use, in what
  order, with what stopping rule?
- Output: a versioned scoping document
  (`docs/sota/scoping_<task>.md`) that becomes the *Method* of any
  resulting inventory.

Analogue: systematic-review protocol / PRISMA pre-registration.

### ii — Plan generation and verification

The agent produces an execution plan: which sources to consult in
what order, what schema to produce, what failure modes to expect.
The plan is *independently verified* (by a different agent or a
panel) **before execution begins**.

This catches the Mitroff-Featheringham Type-III error
(*articulation*, §3 limit #1): solving the wrong problem. Stops
the agent from spending budget on a misguided approach.

### iii — Self-verification (the v1 Exp 2 step)

Single-agent verify-and-polish pass, as locked in 0214 / Annex C.
Already operational; Exp 2 (ticket 0199) measures this.

### iv — Panel of expert verifiers

The v2 sketch from 0223. After self-verify, dispatch four
axis-specialised verifiers in parallel (accuracy / coherence /
provenance / temporality), preferably on different models.
Aggregate edits into a single polished inventory.

Open question (from the 0223 smoke): does the panel approach trade
recall for depth? n=1 evidence suggests yes (43 → 26 plants but
fresh searches on the verify pass). Future runs should pre-register
"recall vs depth" as the measured trade-off.

### v — Swarm verification

The deepest step. Two complementary swarms run after the panel:

- **Per-row swarm** — one verifier per table row, each one's job is
  to verify ONE asset's internal consistency (name, capacity, fuel,
  technology, status, COD, owner, sources). Cell-level granularity.
- **Per-source swarm** — one verifier per cited source, each one's
  job is to confirm that the source actually supports the claims
  attributed to it. The strong-citation test, applied
  exhaustively. External coherence between inventory and sources.

Cost scales with #rows × #sources, not constant. Likely $10s–$100s
per inventory at scale; appropriate only when the dataset is itself
the deliverable (§5), not when it's an experimental measurement (§4).

### Cross-cutting design ideas (author, 2026-05-22)

These two ideas apply across steps i–v, not to any single step:

**A. Workflow-level upfront verification.** Before steps i–v execute,
the agent can verify the *entire workflow itself* — review the
scoping document + planned schema + chosen sources + intended
sub-steps as a whole, and surface inconsistencies *before* any
compute is spent. This is meta: not verifying an artefact but
verifying the *process producing the artefact*. Analogue:
research-grant peer review on a proposal, before the experiment is
run. Cheap; catches Type-III errors (Mitroff–Featheringham) at the
planning stage.

**B. Verification as confidence classification, not binary
filtering.** Rephrase every verifier's output from
*pass/fail* to *IPCC-style confidence band*: virtually certain
(>99%), very likely (>90%), likely (>66%), about as likely as not
(33–66%), unlikely (<33%), very unlikely (<10%), exceptionally
unlikely (<1%). Each verified claim carries its band; the
downstream consumer (e.g. the agent's polish step, or the final
manuscript footnote) decides what to do with each band rather than
discarding weak signals. Preserves epistemic information that
binary verification destroys. This is a vocabulary the harness
should adopt across all five steps and all four §2 axes.

The 0224.* sub-tickets will incorporate both ideas as defaults, not
addenda.

### Scrub of the current workflow against idea B (2026-05-22)

Sites where the current Exp 2 / Annex C workflow uses binary
verification and where IPCC-style confidence bands would slot in:

| Site | Current shape | IPCC-band reframe |
|---|---|---|
| `dialogue_classifier.py` | `report` / `no_report` | 3+ bands so "partial report" can route to a different reply slot |
| Annex C VERIFY string | "verify and polish" without confidence attribution | ask the agent to attach a confidence band to every table row (new column) |
| Phase C cross-eval rubric (0171) | 0–1 scalar per dimension | band per dimension; preserves disagreement strength |
| Strong-citation test (0218) | binary URL-supports-claim | "fully / partially / weakly / contradicts" |
| Row *as-of date* (Temporality) | present / absent | band on the as-of itself |
| Row lifecycle status | discrete category | band on the category (e.g. "operational, very likely") |

The biggest immediate lever is the **VERIFY string** — asking the
agent to attach bands during the polish pass propagates bands into
every artefact downstream. A v3 VERIFY (separate from the deferred
v2 panel approach) is the cheapest concrete implementation of
idea B and could land before §5 / IDH work begins.

## Step ordering and triggers

```
   i. Scoping study    (offline; before any agent call)
        ↓
  ii. Plan + verify    (cheap; gates expensive execution)
        ↓
       EXECUTION (the agent produces the inventory)
        ↓
 iii. Self-verify      (current Exp 2 step; one polish round)
        ↓
  iv. Panel-of-four    (axis-specialised, parallel; recall × depth trade-off)
        ↓
   v. Swarm verify     (per-row + per-source; expensive)
        ↓
       PUBLISH / shipped artefact
```

Each step has an explicit gate to the next. Step i may say "no
inventory is feasible at this quality bar" and abort. Step ii may
say "the plan is wrong". Each post-execution step may say "more
work needed, loop back to execution". The pipeline is iterative,
not linear.

## What this is NOT

- Not a replacement for §1 / §4 / §5 as experimental measurements.
  Those measure *what current systems do*. The IDH cycle is the
  *target system* §5 builds toward.
- Not an immediate implementation. The Exp 2 conference push uses
  step iii only. Steps i, ii, iv, v are post-conference design
  work.
- Not a fixed policy. The five steps may compose differently for
  different tasks (e.g. event lists vs structured inventories).

## Sub-tickets (to file when each step ratifies)

- 0224.A — bibliographic scoping protocol (analogue to PRISMA)
- 0224.B — plan generation + plan-verifier prompt + harness
- 0224.C — re-explore the panel-of-four prompt with measured
  recall × depth trade-off
- 0224.D — per-row swarm design (granularity, aggregation,
  cost-per-row caps)
- 0224.E — per-source swarm design (strong-citation harness,
  URL-resolver, claim-matching)
- 0224.F — end-to-end IDH-cycle integration test on a small
  asset class (e.g. coal plants only, ~50 rows) before scaling

## Exit criteria

This ticket has **no operational exit criteria** — it is design
dialogue. The exit criterion is "author has read this, decided
which sub-tickets to ratify, and either filed them or marked the
remaining work non-priority." After ratification, this ticket can
close as "design sketch, sub-tickets carry the work."

